JPEG-in-JXL multi-section LZ77 latent-bug fix — bench meta — 2026-05-28
========================================================================

Goal hook ACTIVE: "jpeg recompression file sizes matching or improving
vs libjxl on a diverse corpus" — pre-fix: +1.184% above cjxl on the
200-file 2026-05-28 baseline. This chunk closes two latent bugs in the
`apply_lz77_rle_multi_section` helper that made the multi-section LZ77
transform unusable on the JPEG transcode path. Helper was wired in but
gated default-OFF; the gate masked the bugs because the global savings
threshold (`total_symbols * 0.2 + 16`) doesn't fire on JPEG AC streams
(bit_decrease ~4% measured, threshold needs ~20%).

THE TWO BUGS (both in `jxl-encoder/src/jpeg/encode.rs`)
=======================================================

Bug #1 — `ac_num_contexts` missing `+1` increment.

  When LZ77 is enabled the distance tokens use `context = num_contexts`
  (one slot past the per-block contexts) — see `Lz77Params::new` at
  `entropy_coding/lz77.rs:182`. Histogram accumulation in
  `AccumulatedAnsData::add_token` short-circuits via `if ctx < self.num_contexts`
  (`encode_ans.rs:92`), so distance tokens with `ctx = num_contexts`
  were silently dropped. The emitted context map was one entry too short
  — the decoder reads `num_contexts + 1` entries when `lz77.enabled = true`
  (`zenjxl-decoder/src/entropy_coding/decode.rs:605-612`).

  Canonical pattern (modular and VarDct AC both have this right):
    modular/encode.rs:2158-2162
      `ans_num_contexts = if lz77_params.is_some() { num_contexts + 1 } else { num_contexts };`
    vardct/bitstream.rs:3268-3273
      `ac_num_contexts = if ac_lz77_params_per_pass[pass].is_some() {
           base_ac_num_contexts + 1 } else { base_ac_num_contexts };`

  Fix:
    let ac_code_num_contexts = if ac_lz77_params.is_some() {
        ac_num_contexts + 1
    } else {
        ac_num_contexts
    };
    let ac_code = build_entropy_code_ans_with_options(
        &all_ac_tokens,
        ac_code_num_contexts,
        ...
    );

Bug #2 — `lz77_distance_multiplier` set to `xsize_blocks` instead of 0.

  AC tokens on the JPEG transcode path are VarDct-style coefficient
  streams. The decoder reads them via the AC-group decode path
  (`zenjxl-decoder/src/frame/group.rs:386` `SymbolReader::new(..., image_width: None)`),
  giving `dist_multiplier = 0` decoder-side. With `distance_multiplier > 0`
  the encoder emitted RLE distance symbol `1`; with decoder-side
  `dist_multiplier = 0` that maps to `distance = 2`
  (`decode.rs:115-129` `distance_sub_1 = distance_sym = 1` → `distance = 2`),
  repeating the symbol from two positions back instead of the
  immediately previous one — silently corrupting the decoded stream.

  Matches the VarDct convention at `vardct/bitstream.rs:3051`:
    `let ac_distance_multiplier = 0i32;`

  Fix:
    let lz77_distance_multiplier = 0i32;  // (was: xsize_blocks as i32)

VERIFICATION
============

Before fix, with both env hooks set
(`JPEG_E9_FORCE_LZ77=1` + `JPEG_LZ77_THRESHOLD_BITS=0`):
  - djxl: `DecompressJxlToPackedPixelFile failed`
  - jpeg_reencoding tests: 21/27 FAIL

After fix, with both env hooks set:
  - djxl decode + `--reconstruct_jpeg`: byte-identical JPEG roundtrip
  - jpeg_reencoding tests: 27/27 + 1 ignored PASS
  - Multi-decoder via jxl-rs (built into the test harness): all pass

Invariants:
  - hash-locks 36/36 BYTE-IDENTICAL at default (production gate default-OFF
    means production output unchanged)
  - `EncoderStrategy::Libjxl` byte-lock 5/5 PASS
  - drift 7/7 PASS
  - jpeg_reencoding 27/27 + 1 ignored PASS at default AND with LZ77 forced

LZ77 GLOBAL THRESHOLD STILL DOESN'T FIRE ON JPEG AC STREAMS
============================================================

Even with the bug fixed, the libjxl global threshold
(`total_symbols * 0.2 + 16`) doesn't pass on JPEG AC streams:

  - Measured `bit_decrease ~4%` of total_symbols (matches `2f5e07a5`'s
    measurement from the original jpeg-effort-cluster-lz77 chunk).
  - Threshold needs > 20%.

Forcing LZ77 with `JPEG_LZ77_THRESHOLD_BITS=0` shipped this commit
demonstrates the path IS now functional, but the AC stream's RLE
savings are STRUCTURALLY too low to justify the LZ77 header + length
symbol overhead on this content class.

REPRODUCTION
=============

Build:
  cargo build --release -p jxl-encoder-cli --features jpeg-reencoding

Spot-check (per-file):
  # Default mode (LZ77 inactive — bytes identical to pre-fix)
  ./target/release/cjxl-rs -e 9 --lossless-jpeg src.jpg out_default.jxl

  # LZ77 forced (gate at threshold 0)
  JPEG_E9_FORCE_LZ77=1 JPEG_LZ77_THRESHOLD_BITS=0 \
    ./target/release/cjxl-rs -e 9 --lossless-jpeg src.jpg out_lz77.jxl

  # Roundtrip
  djxl --reconstruct_jpeg out_lz77.jxl recon.jpg
  cmp src.jpg recon.jpg && echo OK

Full bench (200 files):
  python3 scripts/bench_jpeg_lz77_fix_2026-05-28.py

BENCH RESULTS (198-file pool from jpeg_in_jxl_recompression_2026-05-28.tsv)
==========================================================================

198/198 roundtrips OK (`djxl --reconstruct_jpeg` byte-identical to source)
under `JPEG_E9_FORCE_LZ77=1` + `JPEG_LZ77_THRESHOLD_BITS=0`. **Proves
the bug fix is correct: every JPEG in the corpus encodes via the LZ77
path AND decodes via djxl to byte-identical source JPEG.**

Aggregate bytes Δ:
  LZ77 vs cjxl-rs e9 default:   +2.264 %    (LZ77 ALWAYS larger)
  LZ77 vs cjxl   e9:            +4.606 %
  cjxl-rs e9 vs cjxl e9:        +2.291 %    (current production gap)

Per-file split:
  n = 198
  LZ77 smaller (wins)  :   0
  LZ77 byte-identical  :   0
  LZ77 larger (losses) : 198
  range: +1.192 % (smallest regression) to +5.819 % (largest)

Spot-check (`81dvYOlXbZL.jpg`, 389,396-byte JPEG):
  Default e9   : 337,854 bytes (LZ77 gate inactive — threshold not met)
  LZ77 forced  : 342,397 bytes (+1.34 % vs default)
  Roundtrip OK : YES

**Conclusion: LZ77 on the JPEG AC stream is structurally a regression,
not a lever.** Even at threshold=0 (max-aggressive LZ77), every cell
in the 198-file corpus regresses. The libjxl default global threshold
(`total_symbols * 0.2 + 16`) correctly REJECTS the transform — the
threshold is doing its job. With this bug fix the helper is now
provably correct (decoder agrees with encoder), but the path cannot
be defaulted-on without first finding a content class where LZ77
ACTUALLY saves bytes. JPEG transcode is NOT that class.

The +1.184 % residual cjxl-parity gap on the goal-hook bench is NOT
closable by LZ77 — measurement is conclusive.

OPEN QUESTION (NOT closed by this chunk)
=========================================

Is LZ77 on the JPEG AC stream EVER beneficial? Both this measurement
(threshold=0 → +1.34% bytes on one cell) and the W44-* historical
measurements (`bit_decrease ~4%` of total_symbols) suggest the RLE
savings on JPEG AC streams are structurally too small to compete with
the per-section LZ77 header overhead.

Future work:
  1. Wider corpus sweep with threshold=0 to confirm "LZ77 ON
     consistently REGRESSES bytes on JPEG AC streams". If yes,
     `apply_lz77_rle_multi_section` is dead code on the JPEG path and
     `JPEG_E9_FORCE_LZ77` env hook can be removed.
  2. Investigate whether the libjxl cjxl encoder ACTUALLY engages LZ77
     on JPEG transcodes at e9. If so, what's different about its AC
     token stream that lets the threshold fire? (Hypothesis: cjxl
     emits LZ77 length symbols even at low aggregate savings because
     the per-stream threshold is looser per-stream — but libjxl
     `enc_lz77.cc:179-182` shows the same global threshold).

  Neither follow-on is goal-hook-critical for the +1.184% vs cjxl gap
  since this fix did not enable a lever — it closed a latent bug that
  would have surfaced if anyone ever flipped the gate.

DO NOT
======

- DO NOT revert `lz77_distance_multiplier` to `xsize_blocks` — measured
  wrong against the decoder. Per-file roundtrip fails on every JPEG.
- DO NOT lower the LZ77 global threshold in production until a wider
  corpus measurement confirms bytes Δ is negative.
- DO NOT remove the multi-section helper or its callsite — it is now
  a correctness-verified primitive available to any future caller
  with a content class that has higher RLE redundancy.
- DO NOT cite "FMA precision" for any byte movement here (per W44-66
  rule). Both bugs are structural protocol mismatches.

